Part 1: the split rho = sigma + rho_r with sigma = phi*phi~ (Lemma R covers sigma at every height)
  sigma = phi*phi~, phi >= 0 piecewise constant on the 0.01 grid of [-1/2,1/2]; sigma and rho are PL on the same nodes,
  so sigma <= rho on [-1,1] iff at the 101 nodes: True (max sigma - rho = 0.0e+00).  kappa_auto(rho) >= int sigma = (int phi)^2 = 0.987962 (CHECKED)
  remainder rho_r = rho - sigma >= 0: int rho_r = 0.024498, rho_r(0) = 2.0e-12, rho_r > 1e-9 only on |a| in [0.01, 0.72], max rho_r = 0.0345 at |a| = 0.21
  sigma(0) = 1.004442 = rho(0); Var(phi) = sigma(0) - int sigma = 0.016479; P(sigma)/int sigma = 1.330655 (>= c_MT = 1.3274993)
  mixtures (column generation, 51 columns, 25 pricing rounds): best mixture mass 0.987962 (NUMERICAL: no gain over the single sigma)
  all-height (**) from Lemma R alone: kappa = 0.987962 -> 2 - P/kappa = 0.662819 (below Montgomery-Taylor 0.6725007)
  [Prop 2.3] any mixture of autocorrelations sigma <= rho has int sigma <= P(rho)/c_MT = 0.995168 < 0.995180 = P/(2 - frontier) needed
             so an all-height argument must extract >= 0.00722 per unit cost (frontier) / 0.01204 (kappa = 1) beyond Lemma R
  [Prop 2.5] high pairs charged at kappa_H = 0.987962, everything else at 1: worst case (2-P-eps)/(1-eps) = 0.670994, eps = 0.02408;
             the frontier is beaten iff kappa_H > 0.99023, or iff the fraction of zeros in high pairs is < 0.2658
